5.text:
In the standard Russian text and in the usual English translations the inspector Ochumelov/Otchumyelov is given a visual tag that is then re-written each time his
social posture to the dog and to the crowd changes.

In the Garnett / Constance Garnett - and in the Russian - the coat/ шинель is not just named, it is re-described at each mood shift:

* on arrival he is presented in his official, self-satisfied uniform, the new/ well-kept coat that marks his authority;
* the first change of opinion is marked by him brushing / worrying at the coat;
* the second change is marked by the coat being shown as dusty / disordered;
* the exit is marked by the coat being again straightened / made presentable as he regains his official pose.

That is the four clearly distinct descriptive passages that are normally quoted as the chameleon-like series. The passages are often condensed in commentary to three “states” – proud/ neat, embarrassed/ dusty, fearful/ fidgety – and the total number of nominal mentions of the шинель/ coat in the story is higher, about six-seven in most editions. The four descriptive re-writings are the ones that are used as the symbol of his fearful mentality and its shifts.

3.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: In the story as it is usually printed the superintendent’s шинель / over-coat is brought into the narrative at every change of colour of the chameleon and the four appearances are the narrative markers of the change.

In the Garnett / most English texts the superintendent first comes out “in his over-coat and cap”, then he is made to take the over-coat off and carry it under his arm when he is anxious to be conciliatory, then he puts the over-coat on again when the political wind changes, and the over-coat is with him again on his departure. That is the four overt mentions of the garment, which are habitually summarized as the three substantive descriptive shifts:

* 1. He arrives in authority in his over-coat;
* 2. He loosens / takes off the over-coat in a show of condescension / fear;
* 3. He buttons / puts the over-coat on again when he re-asserts authority.

That is the three symbolic changes of the coat that stand for Ochumelov’s fearful, chameleon-like mentality, four overt mentions in the text.

