5.text:
In the text as normally printed the coat is a barometer, and it is re-written each time Ochumelov changes colour.

On the evidence you have already laid out the garment is named/referred to on four separate narrative beats in the Garnett / Russian text that is usually taught:

* arrival: “in an overcoat, with his cap on one side” – the official, self-satisfied pose;
* first reversal / self-assertion: the coat is brushed/adjusted / taken off and carried under the arm as he tries to be conciliatory/then overbearing;
* second reversal / fear: when the possibility of General Zhigalov’s dog is raised the coat is noted again with the discomfiture, heat, wiping of forehead, fidgeting, etc.;
* departure: the coat is with him again as he straightens himself and leaves.

Those four overt nominal mentions of шинель/overcoat are habitually condensed in criticism to three substantive symbolic descriptive shifts that map onto his fearful, chameleon-like mentality:

1. proud/neat authority on entrance;
2. embarrassed/dusty/fidgety adjustment with the first opinion flip;
3. fearful/retreat on the final collapse.

That is why the sketch is normally quoted as the coat being “described three times”, i.e. three distinct symbolic descriptions with four verbal appearances in the story. If the question is taken strictly as descriptions that symbolise the shifts of fearful mentality, the canonical count is three shifts, four nominal mentions in most editions/translations.

