3.text:
The coat functions as a barometer, not as an inventory item, and it is re-introduced in the Russian original and in the Garnett / standard English text only when Ochumelov changes colour.

Nominally the shin’el/overcoat is brought back on four narrative beats in the text that is normally taught:

* Entrance in authority: he comes on the square “in his overcoat and cap”, the new/well-kept official garment that marks pompous self-satisfaction;
* First reversal / anxious condescension: the coat is handled/brushed/adjusted/loosened/taken off and carried under the arm as his opinion of the dog/owner shifts;
* Second reversal / craven retreat: the coat is noted again when the possibility that the dog is General Zhigalov’s is raised and Ochumelov becomes discomfited, hot, wiping his forehead and fidgety;
* Departure / exit tag: the coat is with him again as he straightens himself and leaves.

The fourth beat is a presence reference on exit, not a new symbolic re-writing of the garment’s condition.

The three substantive symbolic re-descriptions that criticism consistently quotes as the fearful mentality and its shifts are:

1. proud/neat authority on entrance;
2. embarrassed/dusty/fidgety adjustment on the first opinion flip;
3. fearful/retreat collapse on the General Zhigalov flip.

That is the canonical three-fold descriptive recalibration of the coat that maps onto Ochumelov’s chameleon-like mentality, realised in the sketch as four overt nominal mentions/appearances, with the fourth being an incidental exit note rather than a new descriptive state. If the question is strictly about descriptive symbolic re-writings, the taught count is three.

